1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:04,320
In 1983, the American video game industry collapsed.

2
00:00:04,600 --> 00:00:09,556
In just two years, home video game revenue fell by roughly ninety-seven percent.

3
00:00:09,836 --> 00:00:12,036
Retailers swore off games entirely.

4
00:00:12,316 --> 00:00:15,153
Atari, the company that had defined the medium,

5
00:00:15,153 --> 00:00:19,377
hauled truckloads of unsold cartridges to a landfill in the New Mexico

6
00:00:19,377 --> 00:00:20,765
desert and buried them.

7
00:00:21,515 --> 00:00:25,134
Into that wreckage walked a century-old Japanese playing card company.

8
00:00:25,414 --> 00:00:28,943
Four decades later, look at who else was making game hardware back then.

9
00:00:29,223 --> 00:00:29,735
Atari.

10
00:00:30,015 --> 00:00:30,548
Coleco.

11
00:00:30,828 --> 00:00:31,310
Mattel.

12
00:00:31,590 --> 00:00:32,380
Magnavox.

13
00:00:32,660 --> 00:00:33,272
NEC.

14
00:00:33,552 --> 00:00:34,208
SNK.

15
00:00:34,488 --> 00:00:35,183
Panasonic.

16
00:00:35,463 --> 00:00:35,961
Sega.

17
00:00:36,241 --> 00:00:38,509
Every one of them is out of the console business.

18
00:00:38,789 --> 00:00:40,019
Nintendo is still here.

19
00:00:40,299 --> 00:00:41,568
And it isn't just surviving.

20
00:00:41,848 --> 00:00:45,523
Its last console sold more than a hundred and fifty million units,

21
00:00:45,523 --> 00:00:49,087
and its new one is the fastest-selling machine it has ever made.

22
00:00:49,837 --> 00:00:52,080
So here's a question that sounds strange,

23
00:00:52,080 --> 00:00:53,337
given all that success.

24
00:00:53,617 --> 00:00:55,001
Why hasn't Nintendo died?

25
00:00:55,281 --> 00:00:56,471
Because it really should have.

26
00:00:56,751 --> 00:00:57,605
Several times.

27
00:00:58,355 --> 00:01:03,438
The Virtual Boy, a red-and-black headache machine that sold well under a million units.

28
00:01:03,718 --> 00:01:08,512
The Nintendo 64, which bet on cartridges and lost Final Fantasy to Sony.

29
00:01:08,792 --> 00:01:11,182
The GameCube, which finished a distant third.

30
00:01:11,462 --> 00:01:16,376
The 3DS, whose launch went so badly its price was slashed within six months.

31
00:01:16,656 --> 00:01:20,220
And the Wii U, which sold just thirteen and a half million

32
00:01:20,220 --> 00:01:23,967
units, as Nintendo posted its first annual losses in decades.

33
00:01:24,247 --> 00:01:27,004
For a lot of companies, any one of those would have been fatal.

34
00:01:27,754 --> 00:01:29,535
The usual answer is: Mario.

35
00:01:29,815 --> 00:01:31,742
Great characters, great games.

36
00:01:32,022 --> 00:01:33,809
That's true, but it isn't enough.

37
00:01:34,089 --> 00:01:35,027
Sega had Sonic.

38
00:01:35,307 --> 00:01:37,584
Atari had the most famous brand in the world.

39
00:01:37,864 --> 00:01:40,380
Great games explain why Nintendo succeeds.

40
00:01:40,660 --> 00:01:43,027
They don't explain why it survives its failures.

41
00:01:43,307 --> 00:01:45,868
For that, you have to look at how the company is built.

42
00:01:46,148 --> 00:01:48,072
And when you do, four things stand out.

43
00:01:48,822 --> 00:01:49,280
Part one.

44
00:01:49,560 --> 00:01:51,225
Nintendo is a toy company.

45
00:01:52,575 --> 00:01:56,770
Nintendo was founded in Kyoto in 1889 by Fusajiro Yamauchi,

46
00:01:56,770 --> 00:01:59,969
to make hanafuda, hand-painted playing cards.

47
00:02:00,249 --> 00:02:01,852
For much of the twentieth century,

48
00:02:01,852 --> 00:02:03,925
it was a company searching for a second act.

49
00:02:04,205 --> 00:02:09,433
Under Fusajiro's great-grandson, Hiroshi Yamauchi, it tried a taxi service,

50
00:02:09,433 --> 00:02:12,430
instant rice, and, reportedly, love hotels.

51
00:02:12,710 --> 00:02:13,827
Almost none of it worked.

52
00:02:14,577 --> 00:02:15,674
What did work was toys.

53
00:02:15,954 --> 00:02:21,255
In 1966, a young maintenance engineer named Gunpei Yokoi built an extending

54
00:02:21,255 --> 00:02:23,305
grabber arm to amuse himself.

55
00:02:23,585 --> 00:02:26,644
Yamauchi saw it, and told him to turn it into a product.

56
00:02:26,924 --> 00:02:29,400
The Ultra Hand sold over a million units.

57
00:02:29,680 --> 00:02:32,105
Yokoi was moved into product development,

58
00:02:32,105 --> 00:02:34,590
and Nintendo quietly became a toy company.

59
00:02:35,340 --> 00:02:36,947
That matters more than it sounds.

60
00:02:37,227 --> 00:02:39,703
A toy company doesn't sell specifications.

61
00:02:39,983 --> 00:02:42,526
Nobody buys a yo-yo because of its processor.

62
00:02:42,806 --> 00:02:45,156
A toy has to be understood in seconds,

63
00:02:45,156 --> 00:02:46,701
and be fun in your hands.

64
00:02:46,981 --> 00:02:50,028
That instinct, fun first, explained by touch,

65
00:02:50,028 --> 00:02:53,007
runs straight through the NES, the Game Boy,

66
00:02:53,007 --> 00:02:55,173
the Wii Remote, and the Joy-Con.

67
00:02:55,923 --> 00:02:59,214
When Nintendo brought the NES to America after the crash,

68
00:02:59,214 --> 00:03:01,985
retailers wanted nothing to do with video games.

69
00:03:02,265 --> 00:03:03,814
So Nintendo didn't call it one.

70
00:03:04,094 --> 00:03:06,258
It was the Nintendo Entertainment System,

71
00:03:06,258 --> 00:03:09,108
and it came with a little plastic robot called R.O.B.,

72
00:03:09,108 --> 00:03:11,483
so that wary stores would shelve it as a toy.

73
00:03:11,763 --> 00:03:12,300
It worked.

74
00:03:12,580 --> 00:03:17,221
The NES sold over sixty million units and revived the entire industry.

75
00:03:17,971 --> 00:03:18,515
Part two.

76
00:03:18,795 --> 00:03:20,974
Lateral thinking with withered technology.

77
00:03:22,324 --> 00:03:24,868
Yokoi eventually gave his philosophy a name.

78
00:03:25,148 --> 00:03:27,326
Lateral thinking with withered technology.

79
00:03:27,606 --> 00:03:31,134
Withered means mature: cheap, reliable, well understood.

80
00:03:31,414 --> 00:03:34,300
Lateral means using it in a way nobody expected.

81
00:03:34,580 --> 00:03:38,246
The Game and Watch came from Yokoi watching a bored businessman on

82
00:03:38,246 --> 00:03:40,634
a bullet train, fiddling with a calculator.

83
00:03:40,914 --> 00:03:43,777
Cheap screens, calculator chips, a simple game.

84
00:03:44,057 --> 00:03:46,057
It sold over forty million units.

85
00:03:46,807 --> 00:03:50,438
The Game Boy, in 1989, was the purest example.

86
00:03:50,718 --> 00:03:53,296
Its screen was a murky, greenish monochrome.

87
00:03:53,576 --> 00:03:57,202
Its rivals, the Atari Lynx and later the Sega Game Gear,

88
00:03:57,202 --> 00:03:58,885
had backlit color screens.

89
00:03:59,165 --> 00:04:01,716
They were more advanced in every way you could measure.

90
00:04:01,996 --> 00:04:05,168
They also ate through six double-A batteries in a few hours.

91
00:04:05,448 --> 00:04:08,465
The Game Boy ran for well over ten hours on four,

92
00:04:08,465 --> 00:04:10,435
cost less, and came with Tetris.

93
00:04:10,715 --> 00:04:14,774
With the Game Boy Color, it sold nearly a hundred and nineteen million units.

94
00:04:15,054 --> 00:04:17,512
The Lynx and the Game Gear are footnotes.

95
00:04:18,262 --> 00:04:19,538
The pattern keeps repeating.

96
00:04:19,818 --> 00:04:24,351
In the mid-2000s, Sony and Microsoft were racing toward high definition.

97
00:04:24,631 --> 00:04:27,481
Nintendo's Wii was barely more powerful than the GameCube.

98
00:04:27,761 --> 00:04:31,848
What it had was a motion controller your grandmother could understand in ten seconds.

99
00:04:32,128 --> 00:04:34,424
It sold more than a hundred million units,

100
00:04:34,424 --> 00:04:36,283
and turned up in retirement homes.

101
00:04:36,563 --> 00:04:38,541
The DS did the same for handhelds.

102
00:04:38,821 --> 00:04:42,186
Two screens, a stylus, and games like Brain Age that sold to

103
00:04:42,186 --> 00:04:44,766
people who had never called themselves gamers.

104
00:04:45,046 --> 00:04:47,094
A hundred and fifty-four million units.

105
00:04:47,844 --> 00:04:49,457
The strategic logic is simple.

106
00:04:49,737 --> 00:04:53,122
If you compete on power, you're in a spending war with companies

107
00:04:53,122 --> 00:04:56,295
far bigger than you, and the winner is whoever can afford to

108
00:04:56,295 --> 00:04:57,511
lose money the longest.

109
00:04:57,791 --> 00:04:59,767
If you compete on a new way to play,

110
00:04:59,767 --> 00:05:03,610
there's nobody to outspend you, because nobody else is in that market.

111
00:05:04,360 --> 00:05:04,985
Part three.

112
00:05:05,265 --> 00:05:06,326
The games are the product.

113
00:05:07,676 --> 00:05:11,386
At its core, Nintendo is a software company that happens to build

114
00:05:11,386 --> 00:05:13,213
the machines its software needs.

115
00:05:13,493 --> 00:05:17,229
Shigeru Miyamoto joined as a staff artist in 1977.

116
00:05:17,509 --> 00:05:22,432
One of his first big assignments was to rescue a warehouse of unsold arcade cabinets.

117
00:05:22,712 --> 00:05:25,386
The result was Donkey Kong, and its hero,

118
00:05:25,386 --> 00:05:28,452
a carpenter called Jumpman, would become Mario.

119
00:05:28,732 --> 00:05:31,631
Miyamoto's designs famously start from a feeling.

120
00:05:31,911 --> 00:05:36,124
His childhood memories of exploring the hills and caves around Kyoto became

121
00:05:36,124 --> 00:05:37,248
The Legend of Zelda.

122
00:05:37,998 --> 00:05:39,910
There's a line often attributed to him.

123
00:05:40,190 --> 00:05:42,386
A delayed game is eventually good,

124
00:05:42,386 --> 00:05:44,517
but a rushed game is forever bad.

125
00:05:44,797 --> 00:05:47,075
Whether or not he put it exactly that way,

126
00:05:47,075 --> 00:05:49,135
Nintendo behaves as if it believes it.

127
00:05:49,415 --> 00:05:52,666
Games slip, sometimes for years, until they feel right.

128
00:05:52,946 --> 00:05:55,141
Breath of the Wild was delayed repeatedly.

129
00:05:55,421 --> 00:05:56,328
So was its sequel.

130
00:05:56,608 --> 00:05:58,671
Both were hailed as instant classics.

131
00:05:59,421 --> 00:06:01,580
And because Nintendo makes the hardware,

132
00:06:01,580 --> 00:06:03,092
it designs the two together.

133
00:06:03,372 --> 00:06:08,384
The Nintendo 64's analog stick was developed alongside Super Mario 64.

134
00:06:08,664 --> 00:06:11,081
The Wii Remote and Wii Sports grew up together.

135
00:06:11,361 --> 00:06:15,084
Competitors build a powerful box and invite the world to fill it.

136
00:06:15,364 --> 00:06:18,286
Nintendo builds a toy, and the game that explains it.

137
00:06:19,036 --> 00:06:23,566
Over decades, that produced the most valuable stable of characters in the industry.

138
00:06:23,846 --> 00:06:24,376
Mario.

139
00:06:24,656 --> 00:06:25,115
Zelda.

140
00:06:25,395 --> 00:06:25,939
Pokémon.

141
00:06:26,219 --> 00:06:26,634
Kirby.

142
00:06:26,914 --> 00:06:27,775
Animal Crossing.

143
00:06:28,055 --> 00:06:30,893
Smash Bros. Pokémon, which Nintendo co-owns,

144
00:06:30,893 --> 00:06:34,182
is the highest-grossing media franchise in history.

145
00:06:34,462 --> 00:06:39,348
Mario Kart 8 Deluxe has sold more than sixty million copies on a single console.

146
00:06:39,628 --> 00:06:41,061
And these games don't expire.

147
00:06:41,341 --> 00:06:44,097
Nintendo rarely discounts its own titles deeply,

148
00:06:44,097 --> 00:06:48,175
because a Mario game from six years ago still sells like a new release.

149
00:06:48,925 --> 00:06:51,321
This is the part that makes failure survivable.

150
00:06:51,601 --> 00:06:55,575
When a Nintendo console flops, the people who bought it still got

151
00:06:55,575 --> 00:06:57,348
Mario Kart, Zelda, and Smash.

152
00:06:57,628 --> 00:07:00,667
And those franchises walk straight onto the next machine,

153
00:07:00,667 --> 00:07:02,481
with their fans right behind them.

154
00:07:03,231 --> 00:07:03,841
Part four.

155
00:07:04,121 --> 00:07:04,847
The fortress.

156
00:07:06,197 --> 00:07:09,636
Which brings us to the least glamorous reason Nintendo survives,

157
00:07:09,636 --> 00:07:11,194
and maybe the most important.

158
00:07:11,474 --> 00:07:11,854
Money.

159
00:07:12,134 --> 00:07:16,321
Hiroshi Yamauchi ran Nintendo like a company that expected bad weather.

160
00:07:16,601 --> 00:07:21,103
Unlike its rivals, Nintendo has generally insisted that its hardware sell at

161
00:07:21,103 --> 00:07:23,473
a profit, or close to it, from early on.

162
00:07:23,753 --> 00:07:28,099
Sony and Microsoft can subsidize a console because they have other huge businesses.

163
00:07:28,379 --> 00:07:29,259
Nintendo doesn't.

164
00:07:29,539 --> 00:07:31,634
So every machine has to pay its own way.

165
00:07:31,914 --> 00:07:35,580
It also keeps an enormous pile of cash and essentially no debt.

166
00:07:35,860 --> 00:07:38,439
In good years, critics called that capital lazy.

167
00:07:38,719 --> 00:07:42,169
Then the Wii U happened, and the cash did exactly what it was there for.

168
00:07:42,449 --> 00:07:43,216
It bought time.

169
00:07:43,966 --> 00:07:45,630
Then there's the handheld safety net.

170
00:07:45,910 --> 00:07:50,014
For nearly three decades, Nintendo ran two separate hardware lines.

171
00:07:50,294 --> 00:07:53,597
When the home console stumbled, the handheld carried the company.

172
00:07:53,877 --> 00:07:58,620
The GameCube struggled, but the Game Boy Advance sold over eighty million units.

173
00:07:58,900 --> 00:08:03,008
The Wii U flopped, but the 3DS sold more than seventy-five million.

174
00:08:03,288 --> 00:08:05,176
There was always a second leg to stand on.

175
00:08:05,926 --> 00:08:07,208
Leadership matters here too.

176
00:08:07,488 --> 00:08:10,168
In 2011, after the 3DS price cut,

177
00:08:10,168 --> 00:08:14,228
president Satoru Iwata cut his own salary in half.

178
00:08:14,508 --> 00:08:17,389
When investors later pushed for layoffs, he refused.

179
00:08:17,669 --> 00:08:20,852
His argument was that employees who fear being laid off will not

180
00:08:20,852 --> 00:08:23,488
make software that impresses people around the world.

181
00:08:23,768 --> 00:08:27,222
That kind of decision is only possible if the balance sheet gives

182
00:08:27,222 --> 00:08:28,284
you room to make it.

183
00:08:29,034 --> 00:08:31,330
Now put all four together, and look at the Switch.

184
00:08:32,680 --> 00:08:35,776
By 2017, Nintendo was coming off the Wii U,

185
00:08:35,776 --> 00:08:38,009
its worst-selling home console.

186
00:08:38,289 --> 00:08:41,643
Smartphones were eating the casual market the DS once owned.

187
00:08:41,923 --> 00:08:45,333
Plenty of analysts said it was time to quit hardware and put

188
00:08:45,333 --> 00:08:48,572
Mario on the iPhone, the way Sega had gone software-only.

189
00:08:48,852 --> 00:08:52,379
Instead, Nintendo merged its two hardware lines into one.

190
00:08:52,659 --> 00:08:55,026
The Switch ran on an off-the-shelf mobile chip.

191
00:08:55,306 --> 00:08:56,905
Withered technology, again.

192
00:08:57,185 --> 00:08:59,043
It was a toy you understood in a second.

193
00:08:59,323 --> 00:09:00,975
Pick it up, take it with you,

194
00:09:00,975 --> 00:09:02,513
hand a Joy-Con to a friend.

195
00:09:02,793 --> 00:09:05,152
And it launched alongside Breath of the Wild,

196
00:09:05,152 --> 00:09:07,406
a game that had been allowed to take years.

197
00:09:08,156 --> 00:09:11,152
The Switch went on to sell more than a hundred and fifty

198
00:09:11,152 --> 00:09:14,149
million units, putting it in the same tier as the DS and

199
00:09:14,149 --> 00:09:17,092
the PlayStation 2 among the best-selling consoles ever.

200
00:09:17,372 --> 00:09:21,772
Its successor, the Switch 2, launched in June 2025 and sold three

201
00:09:21,772 --> 00:09:24,345
and a half million units in four days,

202
00:09:24,345 --> 00:09:27,256
the fastest start of any Nintendo hardware.

203
00:09:27,536 --> 00:09:30,364
The company that was told to give up on hardware built one

204
00:09:30,364 --> 00:09:32,998
of the most successful hardware businesses in history.

205
00:09:33,748 --> 00:09:38,361
Nintendo has also learned to take its characters beyond games without giving them away.

206
00:09:38,641 --> 00:09:39,987
Theme parks with Universal.

207
00:09:40,267 --> 00:09:42,655
The Super Mario Bros. Movie in 2023,

208
00:09:42,655 --> 00:09:45,773
which grossed over a billion dollars worldwide.

209
00:09:46,053 --> 00:09:47,867
Each of those is, in the end,

210
00:09:47,867 --> 00:09:49,806
an advertisement for the games.

211
00:09:50,556 --> 00:09:52,038
So is Nintendo invincible?

212
00:09:52,318 --> 00:09:52,687
No.

213
00:09:52,967 --> 00:09:54,574
Its model has real weaknesses.

214
00:09:54,854 --> 00:09:58,039
Every six or seven years, it bets the company on a brand

215
00:09:58,039 --> 00:10:00,769
new idea, and some of those ideas will be wrong.

216
00:10:01,049 --> 00:10:04,071
The same conservatism that protects it also makes it slow.

217
00:10:04,351 --> 00:10:07,061
Its online services have lagged behind for years.

218
00:10:07,341 --> 00:10:09,043
And its hard line on prices,

219
00:10:09,043 --> 00:10:12,142
emulation, and fan projects costs it real goodwill.

220
00:10:12,892 --> 00:10:14,320
But compare the failure modes.

221
00:10:14,600 --> 00:10:18,811
Atari collapsed because it flooded the market with low-quality games and lost

222
00:10:18,811 --> 00:10:19,959
the trust of players.

223
00:10:20,239 --> 00:10:24,038
Sega burned through its cash and its customers' patience with too many

224
00:10:24,038 --> 00:10:27,348
machines in too few years, and couldn't absorb the Dreamcast.

225
00:10:27,628 --> 00:10:32,128
Most of the companies on that list died because one bad bet consumed everything.

226
00:10:32,878 --> 00:10:36,501
Nintendo is built so that a bad bet can't consume everything.

227
00:10:36,781 --> 00:10:38,248
Hardware that pays for itself.

228
00:10:38,528 --> 00:10:39,564
Cash that buys time.

229
00:10:39,844 --> 00:10:42,029
Characters that outlive any console.

230
00:10:42,309 --> 00:10:45,103
And a toy maker's instinct to find a new way to play,

231
00:10:45,103 --> 00:10:47,739
instead of fighting someone bigger on their terms.

232
00:10:48,489 --> 00:10:49,464
That's the real answer.

233
00:10:49,744 --> 00:10:51,723
It isn't that Nintendo doesn't fail.

234
00:10:52,003 --> 00:10:53,032
It fails all the time.

235
00:10:53,312 --> 00:10:55,656
Loudly, publicly, and expensively.

236
00:10:55,936 --> 00:10:57,936
It's that the company is designed,

237
00:10:57,936 --> 00:11:02,526
very deliberately, to survive its failures long enough to reach the next idea.

238
00:11:02,806 --> 00:11:05,488
And every so often, the next idea is the Wii.

239
00:11:05,768 --> 00:11:06,516
Or the DS.

240
00:11:06,796 --> 00:11:07,552
Or the Switch.

241
00:11:08,302 --> 00:11:11,904
A hundred and thirty-seven years after it started selling playing cards,

242
00:11:11,904 --> 00:11:15,905
Nintendo is still in the business of making something fun to hold in your hands.

243
00:11:16,185 --> 00:11:18,769
Everything else is just how it pays for the next one.
